comments.content:
When `execFileSync` times out (the `timeout: timeoutMs` option at line 95), Node.js kills curl with SIGTERM and throws an error where `err.status` is `null` (not `undefined`). The `=== undefined` check on line 108 only catches ENOENT (curl binary missing), so the `null` status falls through to `const code = err.status` on line 111. Since `null === 52` and `null === 56` are both false, the socket is labeled `"refused"` on line 116 — but the daemon didn't refuse the connection; the probe timed out.

Operators seeing `"refused"` will investigate the daemon's socket/bind config instead of load, resource exhaustion, or unresponsiveness — exactly the wrong debugging path.

Suggested fix: add an explicit check for null/kill-signal status before the exit-code comparison, e.g.:
```typescript
if (err.status == null) {
  const signal = (e as { signal?: string }).signal ?? 'unknown';
  attempts.push(`${f}=timeout(signal=${signal})`);
  continue;
}
```
Also update the type annotation on line 106 to include `signal` and `killed` so the fields are accessible without further casts.

