The type-annotation regex `/:\s*[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$<>[\]|]*\s*[=;{]/` matches any colon-followed-by-identifier followed by `=`, `;`, or `{` inside a class body. For example, inside `export class Foo {
  private name: string = "";
}`, the line `name: string = ""` matches the regex (identifier followed by `= {` is not what's tested, but `string =` satisfies `...\s*[=;{]`). Combined with `export type|interface` appearing elsewhere in the body, a simple property initializer in a class that happens to be in a file also containing `export type` will flip `structural` to true, inflating severity to HIGH for what is a MEDIUM logic edit. Consider anchoring the type-annotation regex to appear on the same line as `export type` or `export interface`, or require the annotation to precede a `=`, `;`, or `{` but not within a class body block.

